From 6b28da6ce287bf90ee2bcec248ca64e8e271112c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:55:11 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 113/118] urltest: failures across a change of the network, a
 monitor that sees no route

What a failed dial says about a member depends on what the network did
meanwhile:
- "no such device" (the interface a socket is bound to was removed: a
  PPPoE session that ended, a modem unplugged) is this host's failure,
  as "network is unreachable" is, and waits for the network monitor;
- a failure of a dial, of a write that carries the connect, or of a
  confirming test that began before the network last went, came back or
  was reset is of the network that was, and is not judged. A reset (the
  connections closed, the outbounds told of a new default interface)
  counts as such a change even when the monitor said nothing: Hysteria2
  and TUIC answer every caller that waits for their connection with
  "network changed", and the group moved to another member on that. The
  network manager tells the groups of a reset before it ends anything:
  the outbounds are told one after another, the members before their
  group as a rule;
- a connect cancelled while its caller still waits is this host's doing.

Where the monitor never sees a default route (the routes are in another
table) the pause manager reports no network for good. The report is not
believed while some member holds a URL test result newer than it: the
members are judged from traffic, the ticker of the group runs (the
manager had stopped it, and a member that lost its result was never
tested again), and a new interface forces a round. While no member has
a result at all (sing-box started before the uplink was there, and the
round at start failed) a group in use runs a round itself, an interval
at most: no event would come when the uplink does.

A forced round asked for as the running one ends, after that one has
looked at the flag, is not lost.
